  "facts": [
    "The Eads Bridge, completed in 1874, was the first bridge across the Mississippi south of the Missouri River, pioneering large-scale steel use and setting the stage for future marvels like the Brooklyn Bridge.",
    "Its 520‑foot center arch, the longest rigid span at the time, was built using a cantilever method from temporary wooden towers, a technique later employed in constructing the Brooklyn Bridge.",
    "During its opening, a circus elephant famously crossed the Eads Bridge, proving its safety to a cheering crowd and earning the bridge the nickname “The World’s Eighth Wonder” in the New York Times."
